body: "## Evidence\n\nProbing every traversal subcommand against every stored relationship kind on the shipped 2026.7.26 build, 2026-07-26:\n\n| subcommand | `parent` | `blocked_by` | `implements` | `verifies` | `related` | `discovered_from` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `ancestors` | ok | rejected | **rejected** | **rejected** | rejected | rejected |\n| `descendants` | ok | rejected | **rejected** | **rejected** | rejected | rejected |\n| `predecessors` | rejected | ok | **rejected** | **rejected** | rejected | rejected |\n| `successors` | rejected | ok | **rejected** | **rejected** | rejected | rejected |\n\nThe rejection is explicit and typed: `Relationship kind implements is not a ordering kind` (exit 2, `command_failed`). `ancestors`/`descendants` are hard-scoped to hierarchy kinds; `predecessors`/`successors` are hard-scoped to ordering kinds. Between them, the four traversal verbs cover `parent`, `child`, `blocks`, and `blocked_by` — and nothing else.\n\nThe semantic kinds are reachable, but only through the analytics subcommands. `pm graph impact <id> --kind implements,verifies --direction incoming --max-depth 15` traverses them correctly and returns per-node `distance` and full `path` arrays. `dominators`, `centrality`, `communities`, and `articulation` also accept them. `slack` and `redundancy` reject them; `paths` rejects `--kind` outright with `invalid_argument_value`.\n\n## Why this is a real seam and not a naming quibble\n\nThe typed edge ontology is this epic's deliverable. `implements` and `verifies` exist precisely to express \"this work realizes that outcome\" and \"this gate proves that claim\" — the two questions a planning agent most wants to traverse. After the 2026-07-26 ladder pass the workspace carries 286 `implements` and 91 `verifies` edges connecting 268 of 271 active items to outcome milestones.\n\nAn agent asking \"what implements this milestone\" will reach for `pm graph predecessors <milestone>`. It is the verb whose name means that. It returns 0 to 5 nodes — the ordering-kind answer — with no indication that a semantic answer exists and no hint naming `impact`. The right answer, 16 to 123 nodes at distances up to 5, is behind a subcommand whose name suggests blast radius rather than provenance, and behind a `--direction incoming` flag that has to be known in advance.\n\nThis is the failure mode the whole ontology was built to avoid: the structure is present, correct, and queryable, and the natural query returns a confidently wrong-shaped answer. It is also self-inflicted in a specific way — `pm graph audit` reports `implements` and `verifies` in `edges_by_kind`, so the graph surface advertises kinds that its own traversal verbs will not accept.\n\n## The disposition to take, stated as options\n\n1. **Give the traversal verbs a semantic mode.** `predecessors`/`successors` accept any directed kind; `ancestors`/`descendants` accept any kind whose closure is a partial order. The default kind set stays exactly as today, so no existing invocation changes behaviour, and `--kind implements` starts working where an agent expects it to. Largest ergonomic win, smallest conceptual change.\n2. **Keep the scoping and fix the error.** The rejection message becomes a redirect: `implements is a semantic kind; use pm graph impact <id> --kind implements --direction incoming`. Cheap, honest, and leaves the natural verb permanently wrong.\n3. **Introduce an explicit semantic traversal verb** (`pm graph realizes` / `pm graph proves`). Clearest naming, adds surface to a CLI whose top-level surface this portfolio is actively trying to shrink, so it works against `pm-n7rr`.\n\nRecommendation is (1) with (2) as its fallback message for kinds that genuinely cannot be traversed by a given verb. The reason to prefer (1): the constraint being enforced today is about *closure semantics* — a kind must have an acyclic closure to be an ancestor relation — and that is a property of the kind's declared metadata, not of a hardcoded list. `implements` and `verifies` are both acyclic in this workspace (`pm graph analyze` reports 8 cycles, all pre-existing legacy ordering cycles owned by `pm-hnc9w7`, none introduced by the 286 new typed edges). Deriving the permitted kind set from declared closure metadata is what turns the hardcoded scoping into a rule extensions can satisfy, which is the same derivation argument `pm-w7mqzt` makes for flag contracts.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n- The permitted kind set per traversal subcommand is derived from each kind's declared closure metadata rather than from a hardcoded ordering/hierarchy split, so a package that registers a custom semantic kind gets working traversal without a core change.\n- Default kind sets are unchanged, so every existing invocation returns exactly what it returns today.\n- A kind that genuinely cannot be traversed by a verb produces a redirect naming the subcommand that can traverse it, rather than a bare `is not a ordering kind`.\n- `paths` accepts `--kind`, or documents why it cannot; today it rejects the flag outright while every neighbouring subcommand accepts it.\n- `slack` and `redundancy` get an explicit recorded disposition — supported or documented as ordering-only — rather than failing the same way as an unsupported combination.\n- The docs and `pm guide` state which subcommand answers which question, so `impact --direction incoming` being the provenance query is discoverable without source reading.\n\n## Relationship to neighbours\n\n- `pm-96h7` owns the typed edge ontology; this is the gap between the ontology and the query surface that consumes it. Scoped as a child.\n- `pm-7ob5` builds graph analytics for planning and context — critical paths, blast radius, bottlenecks, explainable relevance. Those all read the semantic kinds, so this item is a precondition for the analytics being reachable through the verbs a planner would use.\n- `pm-4020c5` canonicalises the dependency-kind vocabulary. A derived permitted-kind set only works if the vocabulary is canonical; `related_to` appearing 16 times beside `related`'s 2,111 is the counterexample. Sequenced after it.\n- `pm-bzmeaa` makes bounded outcome reachability an enforced invariant. Its gate will traverse `implements`/`verifies`, so it needs a traversal surface it can call; today it would have to call `impact` and reinterpret the result.\n- `pm-w7mqzt` argues that extension authors cannot consume the SDK's own flag and filter contracts. The hardcoded kind lists here are the same defect in the graph surface: a contract the core keeps to itself.\n- `pm-ju83` (closed) built the SDK relationship registry and graph-query primitives. Recorded as `discovered_from`: the registry supports custom typed edges, and the CLI traversal verbs do not follow it."
